 ## Background
-VoGE (VoGE: A Differentiable Volume Renderer using Gaussian Ellipsoids for Analysis-by-Synthesis) was introduced to provide a differentiable volume rendering approach using Gaussian ellipsoids. It builds on the concept of Gaussian splatting but adapts it for surface representation by flattening 3D Gaussians. The method was demonstrated on CAD models from The Stanford 3D Scanning Repository (Bunny, Dragon, Armadillo) [S1].
+VoGE (VoGE: A Differentiable Volume Renderer using Gaussian Ellipsoids for Analysis-by-Synthesis) was introduced to provide a differentiable volume rendering approach using Gaussian ellipsoids [S1]. It builds on the concept of Gaussian splatting but adapts it for surface representation by flattening 3D Gaussians [S1]. The method was demonstrated on CAD models from The Stanford 3D Scanning Repository, specifically the Bunny (8171 vertices), Dragon (22998 vertices), and Armadillo (33792 vertices) [S1].
 
